# Cross-origin isolation, and it is load-bearing.
#
# The FSRS optimizer is a threaded wasm build, so it needs SharedArrayBuffer,
# which browsers only grant to a cross-origin isolated document. `require-corp`
# is the mode Safari supports.
#
# This file exists because the Nitro `routeRules` that set the same headers
# cannot reach the document on Cloudflare: in SPA mode the page is the
# prerendered `_shell.html`, a *static asset*, and Workers Static Assets serves
# assets before the Worker runs. Without these headers the app still loads and
# nothing errors — `crossOriginIsolated` is simply false, and auto-calibration
# quietly declines to start (see zero/auto-calibration.ts). Check it in the
# console after a deploy: `crossOriginIsolated` must be true.
#
# The cost is that cross-origin subresources without CORP headers stop loading,
# which is why the Google avatar goes through /api/avatar rather than straight
# into an <img>.
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
